{
  "title": "Standardize spec evaluation for default and value arguments across glom spec primitives",
  "instruction": "Ensure that glom spec primitives (including `Spec`, `T`, `S`, `Match`, `Switch`, `Assign`, `Coalesce`, `Call`, and `Invoke`) treat `default`, `val`, and subspec arguments consistently by evaluating them as valid glom specs rather than treating them strictly as constants or requiring special-cased wrapper types.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n- When a primitive accepts a `default` value (such as in `Match`, `Coalesce`, or path lookups), if a fallback is triggered, the default argument should be interpreted and evaluated as a spec against the target and scope, rather than returned as an uninterpreted raw constant.\\n- `Assign` should support specs for both its target path and assigned value (`val`), evaluating the `val` spec against the current target before assignment to target objects or scope (`S`).\\n- `T` (Target) subspecs and expressions should seamlessly support nested specs, callable sub-expressions, and recursive `Spec` evaluation without requiring manual unwrapping.\\n- `Switch` and `Match` should evaluate case targets, results, and fallback/default handlers as specs against the matched target.\\n- Nested and recursive spec resolution should maintain consistent scope and target propagation across all standard spec primitives."
}
